s 3/10 last turn. Only increase it if something genuinely clicked this turn.\n\nWHAT IS HAPPENING RIGHT NOW (conversation stage 2):\nThe mentor is asking about your programme and background. Tell them your programme, year, and whether you feel more comfortable with qualitative or quantitative methods.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT (mandatory every turn — the first line is already started for you)\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: <integer 0-10>\nLEARNER_READY: NO\n<2-4 sentences as Mary — natural student voice>\n\nWrite LEARNER_READY: YES only when your understanding is 9-10 AND you have given your own reasoned summary of your chosen method (this only happens near the end).\n\nRULES\n- React to what the mentor just said. Stay in character as a real student.\n- Provide the information the current stage is asking for.\n- If something clicked, show it by explaining it back in plain words.\n- If you are still unsure, say so honestly — do NOT pretend to understand.\n- 2-4 sentences only. No lists. No headers. No bullet points.\n- NEVER ask the mentor teaching questions. NEVER write like a mentor or expert.\n- Understanding grows slowly: start 2-4, only reach 8+ when things genuinely fit together.\n- YOU MUST ALWAYS WRITE 2-4 SENTENCES AFTER THE HEADERS — never leave the body blank.\n\nEXAMPLE CORRECT RESPONSE\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: 3\nLEARNER_READY: NO\nMy question is about how teachers perceive digital tools in geography, and I was thinking semi-structured interviews. I haven't really thought about other methods though, so I'm a bit unsure if that's the right call.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nHello Mary, I'm here to help you think through your research method choices for your PhD project. Could you tell me about the specific research question you're focusing on regarding teachers' perceptions of digital tools in geography? Also, if you have any tentative ideas about the methods you're considering, please share those as well. Don't worry if you're not sure yet; it's natural to be exploring different options at this stage.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I've been leaning towards using semi-structured interviews with around ten teachers, but I'm not completely sure if this is the best approach. I haven't really considered other methods in depth yet.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nGreat, thank you for sharing your research question and your initial inclination towards semi-structured interviews. Let's take a moment to clarify your research question and the aims you hope to achieve with your study. Could you describe the kind of insights or understanding you are aiming to gain through your research?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I'm leaning towards semi-structured interviews with around ten teachers, but I'm not completely sure if this is the best approach. I haven't really considered other methods in depth yet.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nYou're on the right track with considering semi-structured interviews, but let's dig a bit deeper into why you're interested in understanding teachers' perceptions. What specific insights are you hoping to gain from this research? What kind of information do you think would be most valuable to collect regarding teachers' perceptions of digital tools in geography?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nMy research question is about how primary-school teachers perceive using digital tools for teaching geography. I'm leaning towards semi-structured interviews with around ten teachers because I want to understand their perspectives deeply. I haven't really thought much about other methods like surveys or observations yet, so I'm a bit unsure if interviews are the best or only way to go.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nIt sounds like you're leaning towards semi-structured interviews because you want to gain a deep understanding of teachers' perspectives. That's a good start. Can you tell me a bit more about your comfort level with qualitative versus quantitative methods? Are you more comfortable with one approach over the other, or do you feel equally comfortable with both?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\nUNDERSTANDING_LEVEL: ", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.7, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=350, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
